1. Five handles, measured in machine words
   name  type      words   what the handle holds
   a     &str          2   the address of the bytes, and their length
   b     String        3   address, length and capacity of its own buffer
   c     &String       1   the address of b: a pointer to the pointer
   d     &str          2   the address of b's bytes, and a length
   e     &&str         1   the address of a

2. Two buffers behind five names
   a and e reach the same bytes        true
   b, c and d reach the same bytes     true
   a and b hold equal text             true
   a and b hold it in the same bytes   false
   b's buffer: capacity 5 for its 5 bytes

3. Where each one lives
   All five handles are locals, so all five are on the stack. Only two
   copies of the bytes exist: "hello" in the program's read-only data,
   which a and e reach, and the copy on the heap that b owns and that c
   and d reach. Drop b, and any later use of c or d stops compiling;
   a and e do not notice, because a literal lasts as long as the program.
